<header><p class="st-kicker">protectorate · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Togoland</h1><p class="st-meta">1916 CE – 1960 CE · capital Lome</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The eastern two-thirds of German Togoland, ruled by France under League and UN mandates, which became independent Togo in 1960.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Allied conquest of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/german-togoland">German Togoland</a> in 1914, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> took the larger eastern part, including the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lome">Lome</a> and the railways, in the division of 1916. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> confirmed it as a French mandate in 1922, and after 1946 it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> governed the territory separately from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa</a>, though often with shared officials, and promoted cocoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phosphate">phosphates</a>. Its trust status allowed Togolese to petition the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/committee-of-togolese-unity">Committee of Togolese Unity</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylvanus-olympio">Sylvanus Olympio</a>, campaigned for independence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewe-people">Ewe</a> unity, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-grunitzky">Nicolas Grunitzky</a> led parties closer to France.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1956 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> made it an autonomous republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-grunitzky">Grunitzky</a> as prime minister, but UN-supervised elections in 1958 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylvanus-olympio">Olympio</a> to power. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/togo">Togo</a> became independent on 27 April 1960, and Olympio was assassinated in 1963.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-togoland.jpg" alt="French Togoland" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Original: UnknownUnknown Vector: Eyesnore · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_French_Togoland_(1956%E2%80%931960).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1916 CE – 1960 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>protectorate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lome</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Nicolas Grunitzky, Sylvanus Olympio</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion, Christianity, Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Ewe, Kabiye</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/togo">Togo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Togo under French Mandate, French Togo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=french-togoland" aria-label="French Togoland on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4714 1948 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Togoland lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5034 2204L5034 2204M5034 2204L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5034 2204h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5034 2204h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5034 2204h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lome. <a href="/atlas?year=1938&amp;focus=french-togoland">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
